    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Считают, что человек – хозяин судьбы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They say we are masters of our fate.' The question word for 'what' comes right at the start, followed directly by 'fate'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы сами всё решаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We decide everything ourselves.' Max combines 'we' with 'decide' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы решаем, с кем нам быть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We decide who we want to be with.' Max links 'we' directly with 'decide' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Всё зависит только от нас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Everything depends only on us.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'everything' with 'us'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но судьба преподносит нам сюрприз.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But fate brings us a surprise.' Notice how 'but' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'surprise'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Когда мы начинаем в это верить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'When we start believing in this.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'when' with 'to believe'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Она делает это обычно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It usually does this.' Max links 'it' directly with 'does' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Хорошо, Тимур.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'All right, Timur.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'good' with 'Timur'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Привези мне мясо ягнёнка.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Bring me some lamb meat.' Notice how 'to bring' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'lamb'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Молодого ягнёнка, а не пенсионера.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'A young lamb, not a pensioner.' Chef Viktor combines 'young' with 'pensioner' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Не как вчера.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Not like yesterday.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'not' with 'yesterday'.",
      "gap": 0.4
